      "content": "I hear you, but I'm not sure if that's the most helpful way to look at it. When we're trying to allocate limited resources, we usually have to account for environmental stressors. Even with the same stats, a student's home environment or neighborhood can impact how much \"support\" they actually need to stay on track. \n\nLooking at the zip codes again, do you think the difference in socioeconomic background between 90210 and 90001 should change the risk assessment? Like, if we're looking at long-term stability, shouldn't we prioritize the student in the more \"at-risk\" area?",
